Credential input validation and storage limits
==============================================

Partial credential PUT requests may omit name, credential_type, scope,
is_default and options to leave their values unchanged. Explicit null now
returns HTTP 422 field validation before the endpoint can write. Previously
null required scalar values reached PostgreSQL constraints and returned 500.
More seriously, JSON null options were committed before response validation
failed; subsequent reads of the affected credential also returned 500.
The correction rejects the request without changing the stored record.

Vendor strings are limited to 100 characters and usernames to 255 on create
and update, matching their PostgreSQL columns. The former 128/512 schema limits
allowed values that failed during persistence. Unicode character boundaries,
existing accepted values, false defaults and omitted fields retain their
semantics. Descriptions, site bindings, usernames, vendors and secret inputs
remain nullable where clearing is supported. No new permission is granted.

OpenAPI changes are limited to nine properties in the core credential create
and update request models. The generated core update type marks the five
required values as optional but non-null. Access-control module credentials
are a separate namespaced schema and are unchanged. Enum definitions, response
contracts and unrelated models are unchanged. Frontend lint, tests and strict
build are required again for the regenerated client contract.

Qualification uses actual PostgreSQL rows, HTTP-ASGI requests and separate
application processes over loopback TCP. Every write is followed by a read and
stored-state inspection. The old options-null case demonstrates both the
committed invalid JSON value and the subsequent read failure; the corrected
case verifies 422, unchanged stored values and a successful follow-up read.
Synthetic authenticated users are reloaded per request to avoid confusing
fixture ORM expiration with application failures. No real login or device
operation is implied. Shipping/runtime checks have separate recorded gates.

There is no database migration or historical-data rewrite in this correction.
It does not repair previously stored invalid JSON values, change SNMP encrypted
storage capacity, qualify all secret-length limits, or establish concurrent
permission/session revocation safety. Those remain separate audit work.
